  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA quiet morning across the area as surface high pressure\ncontinues to build in from the north. This has resulted in clear\nskies and calm to light flow, allowing for chilly temperatures \nand valley fog in some locations. Valley fog is most widespread \nacross southwest VA and central/southern WV where lingering \ncloud cover yesterday helped to trap in low-level moisture \nprior to sunset. Further northeast, dry advection has greatly \nlimited fog coverage to mainly just typical river valley steam \nfog via a significant air / water temperature difference. In \ngeneral, expect fog coverage to level out or even begin to mix \nout a bit towards dawn. Nevertheless, areas of dense fog have \nwarranted an SPS across portions of the forecast area this\nmorning. Temperatures this morning will be coldest across the \nnorthern lowlands and especially northern mountains. A Frost \nAdvisory remains in effect until 9 AM for southeast Randolph and\nall of Pocahontas County, where lows will bottom out in the \nlow/mid 30s across the typical valley cold spots. \n\nSurface high pressure to the north will remain in control across\nthe area today through the weekend, bringing mostly clear skies\namid seasonably cool temperatures. Highs will generally be ~ 5\ndegrees below normal, with upper 60s to low 70s across the\nlowlands. Lows will be on the chilly side, generally 5-10\ndegrees below normal, with river valley fog possible each\nmorning. Forecast soundings today indicate a very dry layer at\n~H850-H700 that will mix down to an extent this afternoon. A \nFrost Advisory for portions of the northern mountains could once\nagain be warranted tonight depending on how low afternoon dew \npoints get, but will hold off and let day shift re-evaluate. \nWhile confidence remains low, a retrograding coastal low could \nresult in a few light showers across the far northeast CWA late \nSunday or Sunday night. Otherwise, dry weather prevails. \n\nA gradual pattern shift begins early next week as a modest upper\nridge begins to shift east over the area. This results in\ncontinued dry weather into at least Wednesday, if not longer.\nLow-end rain chances return later Wednesday onward as a warm \nfront lifts north across the region, with a cold front then \napproaching from the west. Given model discrepancies, confidence\nin timing remains low, with PoPs likely washed out to an extent\nmid to late next week given the varying model solutions. Below \nnormal temperatures on Monday will transition to near normal on \nTuesday, with slightly above on Wednesday courtesy of return flow.",
